text:
Carla: Follow-up to the warmup RFC: I'm proposing a micro-lane model for safetensors loads where the loader exposes soft-priority lanes (fast, background, bulk) and returns short-lived lease tokens. Runtime pins the lease for a request lifetime. Goal: avoid full-file prefetch for large weights while letting tiny layers be copied immediately.

Ben: Love the idea. Two quick asks: 1) keep loader surface minimal so we can support both manifested bundles and plain blobs; 2) make failure semantics explicit (e.g., if a stream errors, what's the fallback?).

Carla: Agreed. Draft API sketch (pseudo-Go):
```
// WeightLoader minimal surface
type Lease struct { ID string /* opaque */ }
func Acquire(layer string, lane string, deadlineMs int64) (Lease, bool, error)
func Stream(layer string) (io.ReadCloser, error)
func Release(lease Lease) error
```
Acquire returns (lease, ready, err). ready==true means immediate in-memory availability.

Maya: Important: lease semantics must support pinning across async work. If we hand out a lease and the runtime migrates the backing shard, the old lease must remain valid for its TTL. That avoids KV-cache mismatches mid-query. TTL should be short (e.g., request duration + 250ms jitter).

Sasha: For tiny layers we should avoid the streaming path entirely. Suggest default inline cutoff 128kB (configurable per-model). Inline loads get ready=true and Acquire returns a lease whose Release is a no-op.

Ben: Telemetry plan: track acquire_latency_ms by lane, stream_open_errors, partial_stream_retries, inline_count, streamed_bytes_by_lane. I'll open INF-2024 to capture metric names. infra-bot: can you create the ticket?

infra-bot: Ticket created: INF-2024 "micro-lane-hydration-metrics" assigned to eng-ml.

Maya: Manifestless support: loader implementations that don't have a manifest should still implement Acquire by performing a small index probe or returning a fallback lease id like "blob:<sha>". The runtime adapter will treat any lease starting with "fallback:" as non-optimizable (no chunk handles).

Carla: Good call. Also: scheduling policy. I want lanes to be hints, not strict guarantees. Runtime scheduler can promote or demote lanes based on system pressure. But we must expose when a lane was honored vs downgraded in the lease metadata so consumers can see fallback rates.

Sasha: Placement implication: when we shard weights across devices, co-locate hot lanes' shards to reduce cross-node fetches. The lease metadata should include a placement_hint object (string) the runtime can use in placement decisions. e.g., "placement_hint":"gpu-pool-3".

Ben: Failure and graceful degradation: if Stream fails mid-read, runtime should: 1) attempt fast in-memory fetch for remaining bytes (best-effort), 2) if that fails, fail the request. We should avoid retry storms; add a per-request backoff and metric for partial_fallback_success.

Maya: One more: compatibility shim. We'll provide an adapter that implements Acquire by calling the legacy WeightSource.Fetch and materializing a temp lease token. That keeps old backends supported. Pseudocode: 
```
async function acquireShim(layer, lane, deadline){
  try { return await newLoader.Acquire(layer,lane,deadline); }
  catch(e){
    const r = await oldSource.Fetch(layer);
    // if r.size < inline_cutoff, buffer to memory
    return { ID: `legacy:${layer}:${sha(r)}`, ready: r.size <= inline_cutoff };
  }
}
```

Carla: Ok I'll put together a short RFC addendum with: API spec (Acquire/Stream/Release), lease TTL and CAS notes, inline cutoff defaults, telemetry names, and migration shim. Planning sync Friday 11am. Any objections?

Ben: No objections. Please include unit tests for shim behavior.

Sasha: Thumbs up.

Maya: Sounds good to me.

Carla: Thanks all — filing PR after the sync.
